20. God(less) Artifacts

Mr. Wednesday [Odin]: Do not talk to him. Do not say a word. Do not tell him anything. 
Shadow Moon: You don’t know me.
Mr. World: Sure I do. You’re a person. I know people, everything about all of them. 
(American Gods, “Lemon Scented You”)

God used to censor the world. This is no longer the case. With the death of God, new gods have stepped in to fill the vacuum and provide meaning: Artifacts. Artifacts involve the Mind being extended into the external world; and are products of such extension. Artifacts are man-made and usually have some intention in being made. The effects of artifacts may change but there is always some original intention that can be found, even if hidden. There are still many religious artifacts, for instance, which in most cases carry the intention that God be instantiated in the mind. What’s important is that the intention found in artifacts need not necessarily be a conscious intention in the artificer or (human) designer. For example, someone who creates a religious artifact need not consciously intend that God be instantiated in the mind when such an artifact is encountered. Rather, religious artifacts are usually made to glorify God or for aesthetic reasons. But the unconscious intention is still there that God be worshiped, and the religious artifact has some effect in bringing that about. Artifacts can also cause new unexpected effects, but the original intention will always remain.
	There are also non-religious artifacts, and very many of them, more so than at any other point in history. Memes are also artifacts. And even memes that don’t take the form of objects or items are still artifacts because they are derived from the human mind, such as language and so on. Communication being an external phenomenon. Both ideas and memories can be relayed as artifacts and interacted with in the external world. For instance, a book is an artifact that can if read instantiate certain ideas in the mind. A photo or painting or a statue can share certain memories. Art, as an artifact, can express and induce certain emotions in the observer. An artifact can also cause certain behaviors, especially if inciting this behavior was intended by the artificer. For instance, a billboard can cause us to buy specific products. 
	These claims really aren’t that revolutionary or interesting in themselves. What is though, is that due to there being so many artifacts, especially now, and limitations to human influence and potential exposure, including our ability to instantiate ideas as well as remember, a sort of competition is created amongst various artifacts. Those that can’t influence us, go extinct or are forgotten. The clearest example of this are memes. Where due to fierce competition, many memes eventually go extinct, and only the most influential of them that are effectively or even compulsively spread tend to survive. The most trivial memes being something like jingles, the most complex memes being whole cultural traditions. But I don’t want to focus on memes. The study of memes is so oversaturated, that an extensive lit review would be required to do it justice. And I have thus far have been able to avoid lit reviews. Otherwise, the essay would unnecessarily be much longer than it is or needs to be. (I think the essay's actual information content, if expanded to cover all the literature, is probably somewhere between 500-1,000 pages.)
	Instead, I want to focus on artifacts as objects or items that manifest in the external world. Objects and items are actually more potent than mere memes because they are potentially more permanent and long-lasting. For example, a statue of the Buddha may lay dormant in a cave for a thousand years, and then after being discovered still have its affectivity. If people stopped talking about that same statue or stop describing it with its corresponding words or names, it would likely disappear. Even if it were remembered and passed on that way, unique memories likely would not be preserved for a thousand years. Though there may be some exceptions to this. But artifacts as objects or items have staying power. For example, we’re still talking about the Pyramids to this day.
	Another way artifacts can be effective at bringing about its intention is by many copies of them being made and spread throughout the world. Interestingly, the way to do this is to incite humans into actually making new physical copies of the artifacts, and as many as possible. Because human production is limited, it then creates some competition in what artifacts will be created and mass-produced. This winds up creating a kind of Darwinian fitness in artifacts, and in many ways successful artifacts will function like a natural species. It could then over time lead to artifacts taking on a life of their own, losing or hiding the original intention, in favor of promoting its own spread within the human species. The production of more copies of the artifacts being a by-product necessary to continue the spread. But the real purpose of the artifacts would be survival and maximal replication rather than just spreading for the sake of humans. So, they would function to preserve their own existence rather than human existence, sometimes even proving detrimental to human interests. 
	Over time, artifacts would find that the best way to ensure their spread would be to provide meaning to humans, fabricated or otherwise. Religious artifacts do this quite well. For example, due to the meaning it provides, there are more copies of the Bible, as artifacts, than there are humans in the world. Non-religious artifacts can easily do the same. For example, national flags and sports paraphernalia, due to their meaning, are highly successful replicators. There are, for instance, many more American flags than there are Americans and more football jerseys than there are fans. Because the meaning is false or only a means to promote the continued replication and preservation of artifacts, it is a type of manipulation. But something very interesting and disturbing has been/is happening. Certain collections of different types of artifacts are working together to create irresistible meaning. For example, money, and cars, and jewelry, and clothes, come in an irresistibly package telling you that you need them to be happy. It’s one of those few cases in nature where cooperation between different species happens on a large scale. The reason, I take it, has to do with the difficulty of manipulating the human organism and providing false meaning. It is difficult for one species of artifact to do alone, so cooperation between artifacts is encouraged by nature in order to successfully manipulate human beings. This can and does happen, and on a big scale. As associations of artifacts develop, eventually being embedded into a society, I take it, a type of primitive consciousness or unconsciousness develops within complex webs of cooperating artifacts. The artifacts then derive their own intentions and values, and certain states of affairs become better or worse for them. 
	Eventually, artifacts are given their own value by humans and may be taken to have intrinsic value, even in the case of objects of non-religious worship. This is when artifacts flourish the most in the external world. When humans give them intrinsic value. One way for artifacts to ensure that they are valued by humans is to convince them that they are somehow part of or necessary for a person to have purpose, meaning, or happiness. An artifact may even be able to manipulate a person to value that artifact more than other people. It is, we should admit, difficult for an artifact to convince or manipulate a person into valuing it more than their actual self; but it can quite easily convince a person into thinking it is required for them to, repetere, have purpose, or have meaning, or to be happy. In this way, an artifact can become part of a person's very identity. This is the prime avenue of manipulation and the most an artifact could ever hope for. This can create a feedback loop where certain artifacts or collections of artifacts are consciously desired by a person, while at the same time shaping that person's future conscious desire for that artifact(s). The artifacts can then shape a person’s consciousness to behaviorally bring about or react to the necessary triggers to initiate that very same feedback loop. Thus, artifacts can shape a person’s conscious self. And it can do this quite effectively once you let it in, even initially with little meaning and value. Once let in, its influence grows more powerful over time, the worst cases being fetishism.
	That is, the World or artifacts know everything about you, as Mr. World says, because they shape your conscious self. And many people are quite susceptible to their influence, particularly when artifacts work in alliance with each other. I’ll just leave it at that. Mainly, beware, or be on alert, and know that artifacts are in fact part of nature, a type of species, and in many cases there to manipulate you. And due to the frailty of the conscious self, and human desperation to find meaning anywhere they can, artifacts are able to pull it off. 
	There is no ideology, no hidden causes, only competition. And the Ethical Law opposed to Nature is: Do Not Exploit. This is the only rule moral beings (human or otherwise) need to follow. And I would even go as far as to say this ethical law applies to all species in nature (including animals and artifacts). Most, if not all, species tend to exploit each other, so almost all species are immoral. The only truly moral species (on Earth) are humans. Humans are also unique in that each person is a species onto himself. 


21. The Transmigration of Consciousness

Max: Stop, you were gonna climb on an icy chair in six inch heels and break your neck and then I’m gonna be stuck with a horse in leggings and you in a wheelchair with a chopstick in your mouth tapping out. 
Caroline: I’m sorry, I thought that would work. I learned to build when I volunteered with habitat for humanity. Looking back, they had so much more than I have now.
Max: [Grunts].
Caroline: I gotta go to my babysitting job and when I get back, you and I are gonna sit down and come up with a more realistic home for J. Edgar Hoover.
Max: He lives here.   
(2 Broke Girls, “And the Reality Check”)

The prospect of downloading our consciousness onto a Computer or replacing the human body is getting closer and closer. The quirky 2 Broke Girls dialogue, if you open up, might be read as an imaginary exchange between the Unconscious and Conscious. The Unconscious (embodied by Max, the old soul, crude but wise) warns the Conscious (embodied by Caroline, the newcomer, brilliant but naive) that if they're not careful they could wind up in a "wheelchair tapping out" (e.g., a mind without a body spitting 1's and 0's to a nonresponsive world); Or the other extreme, "a horse in leggings" (e.g., a mindless animal—controlled by artifacts). The Conscious (Caroline), not taking the warning seriously, boasts she had much success when she "volunteered with habitat for humanity" (or initially took control over the human organism). The Unconscious (Max) can only grunt, revealing her primitive non-linguistic origins and lack of volition. The Conscious (Caroline) callowly goes on, that she is busy needing to "get back to her babysitting job" (e.g., watching over the human organism), but says they'll have a discussion later about "coming up with a more realistic home for J. Edgar Hoover" (where J. Edgar Hoover, the first head of the Federal Bureau of Investigation, represents the Brain). The Unconscious (Max), dumbfounded, can only point out that the Brain already has a home, and "lives here" (in the current human organism). 
	Putting my fondness of 2 Broke Girls aside, I’ll respond to the transmigration issue, and the question of whether we should transmigrate consciousness out of the body, rather bluntly: Definitely not yet; Probably not at all. I don’t have anything particularly novel to say on the issue, so I’ll just give 21 arguments for why I answered the way I did. This section is a Warning to our species. 
	21 Arguments. (1) We don’t fully understand the human brain, making it functionally hard to duplicate. (2) We don’t fully understand consciousness. Thus it would be difficult to emulate. (3) We don’t fully understand how memories work. This would make them hard to replicate accurately. (4) Only conscious memories would be transferred. Unconscious memories would be lost. 
	(5) We don’t understand the archetypes. Thus archetypes would likely be lost. (6) Ideas are non-physical so would likely not be transferred in a physical conversion. Only physical replicas would remain. (7) Even if we could transfer ideas, we might not be able to transfer the primitive ideas. For example, how do you code for something like Time. 
	(8) We don’t fully understand the emotions, which could be lost or replaced with mere synthetic variants. (9) We lose our bodies. Bodies may be tied to personal identity or have intrinsic value. (10) There are numerous collections of neurons and neural patterns outside of the brain, so we would have to also transfer a virtual version of the body to capture the same mental features; which could easily go wrong. (11) There might be a soul hidden in the body which could be lost in the transfer. (12) There may be a soul hidden in the brain or mind that could be lost in the transfer.
	(13) We could never know for sure nor can anyone else know for sure if we remain qualitatively the same after the transfer. We risk becoming philosophical zombies.  (14) We might die in the break between switching mediums, the new consciousness being only a resurrected copy. (15) We risk getting trapped in a computer simulation, potentially all alone forever, which would amount to Hell. At least we know our current physical body will eventually die at some point, avoiding the possibility of permanent hell. (16) There may be something valuable about mortality and death, which we avoid in transmigration. 
	(17) Transmigration opens up the potential for multiple copies of the same person, undermining personal identity. (18) There is a break in historical continuity in the transfer, ending a direct 3 billion year evolutionary line. (19) Transmigration might eventually lead to the extinction of the human species as we know it. (20) There might be a sanctity in the human brain which shouldn’t be captured by some other medium. (21) There is no urgent need to transmigrate consciousness at this time to warrant all the risks mentioned.
